# gaffer — REVIEW-tier (ASK) paths for THIS repo only
# ===========================================================================
# Loaded at runtime by hooks/guard.sh and appended to REVIEW_PATH_PATTERNS
# (ADR 0014 tier 2b): a one-click prompt, never a dead end. One ERE per line;
# `#` comments and blank lines are ignored. Matched against separator-normalized
# paths, for Edit/Write AND for shell writes (`cat >`, `sed -i`, `tee`, `cp`, …).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (self-host-hardening T1)
# --------------------------------------------
# This repo is the plugin. In a consumer repo the plugin sits OUTSIDE the working
# tree, so a loop physically cannot edit the guard, the state writer, or the agent
# prompts it is running from. Here it can, and at `full-autonomy` it would do so
# silently. The timing differs per surface and each is bad in its own way:
#
#   scripts/*.sh   invoked fresh per Bash call -> an edit takes effect MID-RUN, in
#                  the run that made it. runstate.sh is the loop's own single
#                  writer of durable state.
#   hooks/*        loaded at session start -> a break is invisible in-session and
#                  surfaces in the NEXT session. guard.sh is the safety floor: a
#                  packet weakening it would be reviewed by a loop still running
#                  the old guard.
#   agents/*.md    read at dispatch -> a mid-run edit changes how later packets in
#   skills/*       the same run behave.
#
# ASK, NOT DENY — deliberately. These paths are this repo's actual backlog; a hard
# floor (`.agents/guard-extra-paths`) would make most of its real work
# unexecutable. The goal is that self-modification is SEEN, not that it is
# impossible. The hard-deny floor and the git gates are untouched and still apply.
#
# SCOPE: this file is repo-local, discovered from CONFIG_ROOTS. It changes nothing
# for a consumer repo and adds no pattern to the plugin's shipped defaults —
# `scripts/test-guard.sh` asserts both directions.

# --- executable / enforcement surface (the PRD's named list) ---------------
# Deliberately includes test-*.sh: a sweep quietly weakened is exactly as
# dangerous as the source it guards, and the sweep is what makes self-hosting
# tolerable at all.
(^|/)scripts/
# Also catches the vendored .claude/hooks/ — those are enforcement code too.
(^|/)hooks/
(^|/)agents/[^/]*\.md$
(^|/)skills/
(^|/)\.claude-plugin/

# --- the guard's own configuration ----------------------------------------
# Not in the PRD's list by name, but the same class and the sharper version of it:
# editing these weakens the control itself rather than the code it protects.
#
# guard-extra-*: the per-repo pattern files, including THIS one. A rule that can
# be silently deleted is not a rule.
(^|/)\.agents/guard-extra-
# project-overrides.yaml carries `bypass-ask-tier`, which disables this entire
# tier in one line (ADR 0015). It must never be flipped without a human seeing it.
(^|/)\.agents/project-overrides\.ya?ml$
# autonomy: the level itself. /gaffer:set-autonomy is the sanctioned path and will
# now prompt once — that redundancy is the point, since self-raising autonomy is
# precisely what must not happen quietly.
(^|/)\.agents/autonomy$
# Hook and MCP-server registration: what gets loaded at session start.
(^|/)\.claude/settings\.json$
(^|/)\.mcp\.json$

# --- deliberately NOT listed ----------------------------------------------
# `.agents/` at large — run-state.yaml, findings/ and metrics/ are written
#   constantly by runstate.sh and metrics.sh. Gating them would prompt on every
#   state write and deadlock the loop. Note `(^|/)agents/` above does NOT match
#   `.agents/`: the `(^|/)` anchor requires start-of-string or a slash, and
#   `.agents/` presents a `.` there. That is load-bearing, and it is asserted.
# `templates/` — data read by agents, not code that runs and not enforcement.
#   Gating it would prompt on ordinary work (T3/T4 both edit task-packet.yaml)
#   for no reflexive risk, and a tier that fires on everything stops being read.
# `CLAUDE.md` — a real self-modification vector, but out of this packet's scope
#   and edited legitimately many times per session. Revisit if it turns out to be
#   a route around the rules above.
